WEBVTT

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:07.380
Nine days One person And more than six hundred

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:07.805
A.I.

00:00:07.825 --> 00:00:08.925
agent sessions

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:12.848
This is Warsaw Downtown a multiplayer shooter

00:00:12.867 --> 00:00:15.235
that runs in a web browser built on the

00:00:15.255 --> 00:00:18.255
real streets around Warsaw's Palace of Culture

00:00:18.492 --> 00:00:19.660
Kiran directed the A.I.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.255
agents that wrote the code

00:00:21.812 --> 00:00:23.817
But this isn't a story about how smart the

00:00:23.837 --> 00:00:26.117
agents were It's about the structure that

00:00:26.137 --> 00:00:28.630
kept them honest And that structure works

00:00:28.649 --> 00:00:30.212
for anything you want to build

00:00:34.107 --> 00:00:36.194
First three quick ideas

00:00:36.764 --> 00:00:38.913
A language model is a prediction machine

00:00:39.176 --> 00:00:41.343
Give it words and it predicts the words

00:00:41.364 --> 00:00:43.956
that come next Ask for code and it writes

00:00:43.976 --> 00:00:44.514
code

00:00:45.076 --> 00:00:47.643
But on its own it's a brain in a jar It

00:00:47.663 --> 00:00:50.531
can't open a file run a program or see what

00:00:50.551 --> 00:00:51.089
it made

00:00:52.032 --> 00:00:55.462
Give it tools read edit run look Then put

00:00:55.482 --> 00:00:59.337
it in a loop Think Act Observe Decide what's

00:00:59.357 --> 00:01:00.007
next

00:01:00.584 --> 00:01:04.240
That loop is an agent Not a chatbot A teammate

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.915
that can try something check it and try

00:01:06.934 --> 00:01:07.472
again

00:01:08.392 --> 00:01:11.455
One agent can fix a bug A game takes hundreds

00:01:11.717 --> 00:01:14.167
And hundreds of anything need organizing

00:01:14.417 --> 00:01:15.642
That's what graphs are for

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.492
A graph is dots and lines Dots called nodes

00:01:19.512 --> 00:01:23.517
are things places tasks people Lines called

00:01:23.537 --> 00:01:26.605
edges are relationships Can walk to Must

00:01:26.624 --> 00:01:28.437
finish before Is part of

00:01:28.988 --> 00:01:31.555
Even the game's bots think in graphs To

00:01:31.575 --> 00:01:34.518
reach you a bot searches a web of walkable

00:01:34.538 --> 00:01:36.088
points for the cheapest route

00:01:37.012 --> 00:01:39.892
So two big ideas Agentic engineering is

00:01:39.912 --> 00:01:43.067
designing the loop what an agent knows what

00:01:43.087 --> 00:01:45.475
it may touch and how it checks itself

00:01:46.056 --> 00:01:48.606
Graph engineering is designing the connections

00:01:48.894 --> 00:01:51.506
who hands work to whom what waits for what

00:01:51.844 --> 00:01:54.006
and where the final yes or no gets made

00:01:55.544 --> 00:01:56.982
Here's what that looked like at scale

00:01:57.594 --> 00:02:00.344
Three hundred and sixteen saved checkpoints

00:02:00.606 --> 00:02:02.757
A hundred and five thousand lines of code

00:02:03.082 --> 00:02:05.244
Fifteen hundred automated tests

00:02:05.796 --> 00:02:08.401
One lead agent and two hundred and forty-two

00:02:08.421 --> 00:02:11.276
helpers plus three hundred and seventy sessions

00:02:11.296 --> 00:02:12.496
of a second A.I.

00:02:12.559 --> 00:02:13.384
Codex

00:02:13.976 --> 00:02:17.456
Over six billion tokens processed And ninety-six

00:02:17.476 --> 00:02:20.168
percent of it was re-reading agents going

00:02:20.189 --> 00:02:22.518
back over their notes before every single

00:02:22.538 --> 00:02:23.176
move

00:02:23.727 --> 00:02:26.370
But volume isn't the point A thousand agents

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:29.027
in a bad structure make a thousand messes

00:02:29.077 --> 00:02:31.645
faster Here are eight principles that made

00:02:31.665 --> 00:02:33.927
it work None of them are about games

00:02:36.572 --> 00:02:38.910
One Define done before you start

00:02:39.447 --> 00:02:41.727
An agent will work forever or stop at the

00:02:41.747 --> 00:02:44.052
first thing that looks finished So every

00:02:44.072 --> 00:02:46.772
job had a finish line a machine could check

00:02:47.022 --> 00:02:51.122
Sixty frames per second Original work untouched

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:52.622
Every test passing

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:55.747
Write the finish line down Make it checkable

00:02:57.372 --> 00:02:59.459
Two Context is the map

00:03:00.032 --> 00:03:02.612
Every agent wakes up with no memory That's

00:03:02.632 --> 00:03:04.949
all that re-reading So the project kept

00:03:04.969 --> 00:03:07.857
written rules decisions and open checks

00:03:07.932 --> 00:03:10.282
and every new agent read them first

00:03:10.865 --> 00:03:13.919
Without that map a brilliant agent confidently

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:15.290
builds the wrong thing

00:03:16.962 --> 00:03:19.392
Three Split the work where it naturally

00:03:19.412 --> 00:03:20.437
separates

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:24.605
Rendering rain weapons the market Each became

00:03:24.625 --> 00:03:27.500
its own lane with its own copy of the project

00:03:27.763 --> 00:03:30.480
and a contract What you may change What

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:33.100
you must not touch What proof to bring back

00:03:33.632 --> 00:03:36.387
Up to seven lanes ran at once until the

00:03:36.407 --> 00:03:39.499
laptop ran out of memory And where two lanes

00:03:39.519 --> 00:03:42.737
touched the same file things broke Parallel

00:03:42.757 --> 00:03:45.994
work is powerful Boundaries are its price

00:03:47.674 --> 00:03:49.987
Four Put prerequisites first

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:53.373
Some work can't start until other work exists

00:03:53.635 --> 00:03:55.890
Warsaw's shared game rules feed both the

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:59.185
player's app and the server Draw those arrows

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:01.965
and you know what to build first and what

00:04:01.985 --> 00:04:03.623
breaks when something changes

00:04:05.335 --> 00:04:08.228
Five Never let the builder grade their own

00:04:08.248 --> 00:04:08.810
work

00:04:09.359 --> 00:04:11.764
Every change went to fresh reviewers who

00:04:11.784 --> 00:04:14.264
had never seen the builder's reasoning Five

00:04:14.284 --> 00:04:17.089
specialists plus a visual check side by

00:04:17.109 --> 00:04:17.722
side

00:04:18.309 --> 00:04:21.734
Their findings join Skeptics try to disprove

00:04:21.784 --> 00:04:25.659
each one Then a gate decides Fan out Join

00:04:25.909 --> 00:04:27.683
Challenge Decide

00:04:28.215 --> 00:04:31.215
Of sixteen recorded reviews half ended in

00:04:31.265 --> 00:04:34.146
blocked That's not failure That's the system

00:04:34.166 --> 00:04:34.678
working

00:04:36.516 --> 00:04:39.666
Six And this one bit the project itself

00:04:40.256 --> 00:04:42.886
The review system once read a crashed reviewer

00:04:42.906 --> 00:04:45.311
as a reviewer that found nothing Silence

00:04:45.331 --> 00:04:46.856
counted as approval

00:04:47.428 --> 00:04:49.208
And while the second reviewer was offline

00:04:49.228 --> 00:04:52.046
for three days twenty-three defects slipped

00:04:52.065 --> 00:04:54.290
past a test suite that stayed green

00:04:54.864 --> 00:04:57.744
Now a missing check is an error Never a

00:04:57.764 --> 00:04:58.501
pass

00:05:00.733 --> 00:05:02.920
Seven Measure what you actually mean

00:05:03.476 --> 00:05:05.868
One speed test looked smooth but it was

00:05:05.888 --> 00:05:08.793
only fourteen one-second samples Thrown

00:05:08.813 --> 00:05:12.131
out A network test under lag counted just

00:05:12.151 --> 00:05:15.201
ninety-one percent of shots After a fix

00:05:15.288 --> 00:05:16.713
ninety-nine point two

00:05:17.272 --> 00:05:20.172
Tests check rules Screenshots check looks

00:05:20.422 --> 00:05:22.740
Timings check speed A human playing checks

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:26.347
fun No single number tells the whole truth

00:05:27.939 --> 00:05:30.114
Eight Close the loop with your eyes

00:05:30.697 --> 00:05:32.689
The Palace columns were meant to have carved

00:05:32.709 --> 00:05:35.809
grooves The code said they did On screen

00:05:35.934 --> 00:05:38.296
they were flat hidden behind panels

00:05:38.864 --> 00:05:40.864
Only looking at the real result caught it

00:05:41.127 --> 00:05:45.201
Diagnose fix check again And log every failure

00:05:45.452 --> 00:05:48.669
This project logged forty-six so no agent

00:05:48.689 --> 00:05:49.514
repeats them

00:05:51.439 --> 00:05:53.769
Now notice who sits at the top of every

00:05:53.789 --> 00:05:56.927
one of these graphs Not an agent A person

00:05:57.483 --> 00:06:00.589
Kiran set the taste the scope the quality

00:06:00.608 --> 00:06:03.039
bar and the budget in around a hundred and

00:06:03.059 --> 00:06:05.901
forty messages and decided when the evidence

00:06:05.921 --> 00:06:09.426
was enough Agents multiplied the work The

00:06:09.446 --> 00:06:11.284
human decided what it was for

00:06:13.017 --> 00:06:15.584
Put it together and you get one shape A

00:06:15.604 --> 00:06:18.947
goal A map Lanes with owners Arrows for

00:06:18.966 --> 00:06:21.741
order Checks that are joined challenged

00:06:21.817 --> 00:06:24.166
and gated And a loop back to the start

00:06:24.736 --> 00:06:27.054
That shape built a city It can run a support

00:06:27.074 --> 00:06:30.966
team a research pipeline a back office Agents

00:06:30.986 --> 00:06:34.048
do the work Graphs make the work trustworthy

00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:37.530
Was it perfect No.

00:06:37.805 --> 00:06:40.885
Things broke Reviews missed things Numbers

00:06:40.905 --> 00:06:43.142
lied The structure is what caught them

00:06:43.691 --> 00:06:46.447
Next Kiran wants agents inside the game

00:06:46.466 --> 00:06:49.697
itself bots that reason instead of following

00:06:49.716 --> 00:06:50.479
scripts

00:06:52.028 --> 00:06:54.728
Connect with purpose Check with evidence
